This license covers the site code only: stylesheets, Jekyll layouts and config,
and any scripts. The framework itself — the module structure, the classification
vocabularies and their definitions, the method text, and the case library — is
licensed separately under CC BY 4.0. See LICENSE-CONTENT.

Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International (CC BY 4.0)

Copyright (c) 2026 Gaeun Lim

The framework content in this repository is licensed under the Creative Commons
Attribution 4.0 International License.

  Summary:    https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/
  Legal code: https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode


WHAT THIS COVERS

  - The three-module structure and the reasoning behind it
  - Classification vocabularies and their definitions (Object Type, Collision
    Typology, Firm-Level Traces, Event Type, Transmission, Distribution,
    Re-formation)
  - The tagging template and the definitions of the quantitative fields
  - Method and scope statements
  - The case library in _data/cases/
  - _data/schema.yml, insofar as it encodes the above

This is a split by intent, not by file extension. Framework text lives inside
.html files that are also templates; the prose is CC BY, the markup around it
is MIT. In practice this matters only if someone reuses one without the other.


WHAT THIS DOES NOT COVER

  - Stylesheets, Jekyll layouts, configuration, and scripts — see LICENSE (MIT)
  - Quoted or cited source material: published rules, gazettes, consultation
    records, court filings, and industry comments retain their own terms. This
    license applies to the framework's treatment of those sources, not to the
    sources themselves.


ATTRIBUTION

You are free to share and adapt this material for any purpose, including
commercially, provided you give appropriate credit, link to the license, and
indicate whether changes were made. Suggested form:

  Lim, Gaeun. "How Regulatory Order Reaches Actors: Framework v3."
  https://github.com/GaeunLim01/regulatory-order — CC BY 4.0

If you adapt the framework — adding an Object Type, splitting a Transmission
channel, changing what a tag means — say so. A modified vocabulary that keeps
the original tag names while changing their definitions is the one adaptation
that actively damages the shared record.
